# Line endings are pinned to LF for every tracked file.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (do not remove):
# The four-hour research evaluator hashes a source bundle over this specification,
# everything under server/src/research/fourHour/, server/package.json,
# server/tsconfig.json, and server/jest.config.cjs. That bundle was read from the
# WORKING TREE while core.autocrlf=true was in effect globally, which made
# sourceBundleSha256 a function of (commit x local checkout normalization) rather
# than of the commit alone.
#
# Git's clean filter strips CR before comparing, so `git status` reported a clean
# worktree while server/package.json, server/tsconfig.json and server/jest.config.cjs
# actually held CRLF on disk and LF in the object database.
#
# Two failure modes followed. First, a sealed snapshot would record a source hash
# that nobody could reproduce from the commit on another machine, permanently, in a
# one-shot artifact the specification forbids replacing. Second, and worse: any
# checkout, stash, restore, or branch switch would re-materialise the fourHour
# sources as CRLF, flipping the bundle hash while HEAD and `git status` stayed
# unchanged, at which point assertEvaluatorCompatible would permanently reject an
# already-fetched canonical snapshot and brick the research family.
#
# Pinning LF removes the platform dependence. The evaluator additionally reads the
# bundle from committed git blobs rather than the worktree, so this file is defence
# in depth rather than the only guard.
* text eol=lf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gz binary
*.ico binary
